[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Node.js Util Module: Utility Functions for Developers"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"The 'util' module in Node.js provides a collection of utility functions that are useful for developers. These functions cover a wide range of tasks, including debugging, formatting strings, inspecting objects, and working with asynchronous operations. This tutorial will explore some of the most commonly used functions within the 'util' module."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"Inspecting Objects with util.inspect()"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"The 'util.inspect()' function is a powerful tool for debugging and logging. It allows you to convert any JavaScript object into a string representation, making it easy to examine its properties and values. It's especially useful for complex objects with nested structures."},{"order":5,"type":"code","text":"const util = require('util');

const obj = {
  a: 1,
  b: { c: 2, d: [3, 4] },
  e: 'hello'
};

console.log(util.inspect(obj, { showHidden: false, depth: null, colors: true }));"},{"order":6,"type":"paragraph","text":"In the example above, 'util.inspect()' is used to display the object 'obj'. The 'showHidden' option controls whether non-enumerable properties are included. 'depth' specifies the maximum recursion depth (null for unlimited). 'colors' enables syntax highlighting in the output."},{"order":7,"type":"subheading","text":"String Formatting with util.format()"},{"order":8,"type":"paragraph","text":"The 'util.format()' function provides a way to format strings similar to 'printf' in C. It allows you to insert values into a string using format specifiers."},{"order":9,"type":"code","text":"const util = require('util');

const name = 'Alice';
const age = 30;

const formattedString = util.format('Hello, %s! You are %d years old.', name, age);
console.log(formattedString); // Output: Hello, Alice! You are 30 years old."},{"order":10,"type":"paragraph","text":"Common format specifiers include '%s' for strings, '%d' for numbers, '%j' for JSON, and '%%' for a literal '%'."},{"order":11,"type":"subheading","text":"Deprecation Warnings with util.deprecate()"},{"order":12,"type":"paragraph","text":"The 'util.deprecate()' function is used to mark functions or methods as deprecated. When a deprecated function is called, a warning message is printed to the console."},{"order":13,"type":"code","text":"const util = require('util');

function oldFunction() {
  console.log('This function is old.');
}

const deprecatedFunction = util.deprecate(oldFunction, 'oldFunction() is deprecated. Use newFunction() instead.');

deprecatedFunction(); // Prints a deprecation warning along with the console log."},{"order":14,"type":"paragraph","text":"This helps developers identify and migrate away from outdated code."},{"order":15,"type":"subheading","text":"Promisifying Callback-Based Functions with util.promisify()"},{"order":16,"type":"paragraph","text":"The 'util.promisify()' function converts callback-based functions into promise-based functions, making it easier to work with asynchronous code using 'async/await'."},{"order":17,"type":"code","text":"const util = require('util');
const fs = require('fs');

const readFilePromise = util.promisify(fs.readFile);

async function readMyFile(filename) {
  try {
    const data = await readFilePromise(filename, 'utf8');
    console.log(data);
  } catch (err) {
    console.error('Error reading file:', err);
  }
}

readMyFile('myFile.txt');"},{"order":18,"type":"paragraph","text":"In this example, 'fs.readFile' is promisified, allowing you to use 'await' to read the file asynchronously."},{"order":19,"type":"subheading","text":"Conclusion"},{"order":20,"type":"paragraph","text":"The 'util' module provides a valuable set of utility functions for Node.js developers. By understanding and utilizing functions like 'util.inspect()', 'util.format()', 'util.deprecate()', and 'util.promisify()', you can improve your debugging workflow, format strings effectively, manage deprecated code, and simplify asynchronous operations."}]
